Q1.Difference between Abstract Class and Interface with exampleAbstract ClassInterfaceKeywordabstract classinterfaceMethodsAbstract + Normal methods irukalamJava 8 ku munadi full abstract. Ippo default/static method varalamVariablesNormal + final variablesOnly public static final constantsInheritanceextends - oru class mattumimplements - neraya interface implement pannalamAccess Specifierpublic, protected, private use pannalamFull public dhanObjectObject create panna mudiyadhuObject create panna mudiyadhuJava Example:java// Abstract Class
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound(); // abstract method
    
    void sleep() { // normal method
        System.out.println("Animal is sleeping");
    }
}

// Interface
interface Vehicle {
    void start(); // by default public abstract
    void stop();
    
    default void horn() { // default method
        System.out.println("Beep Beep");
    }
}

// Implementation
class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

class Car implements Vehicle {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }
    public void stop() {
        System.out.println("Car stopped");
    }
}
Q2. Explain Overloading and Overriding with exampleMethod OverloadingMethod OverridingMeaningSame class la same name, different parametersParent & Child class la same name, same parametersBindingCompile time PolymorphismRun time PolymorphismPurposeSame work ah different data ku panradhuParent method ah child la change panradhuJava Example:javaclass Calculator {
    // 1. METHOD OVERLOADING - same name, different parameters
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    double add(double a, double b) { // parameter type different
        return a + b;
    }
    
    int add(int a, int b, int c) { // parameter count different
        return a + b + c;
    }
}

// 2. METHOD OVERRIDING - Parent & Child
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override  // annotation
    void sound() {  // same name, same parameters
        System.out.println("Meow Meow");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Overloading test
        Calculator c = new Calculator();
        System.out.println(c.add(2, 3));       // 5
        System.out.println(c.add(2.5, 3.5));   // 6.0
        System.out.println(c.add(1, 2, 3));    // 6
        
        // Overriding test
        Animal a = new Cat(); // Upcasting
        a.sound(); // Output: Meow Meow - Child method run aagum
    }
}
